.SH NAME
csplit \- split file according to contextual arguments
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B csplit
[ \fB\-f \fIprefix\fR ]
.RB [ \-k ]
.RB [ \-s ]
\fIfile first_arg [\s+3.\|.\|.\s0 last_arg]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Csplit
reads
.I file
and separates it into number-of-arguments +1 sections,
defined by the arguments
.IR first_arg "\s+3.\|.\|.\s0"
.IR last_arg .
By default the sections are placed in
\fIxx\fR00 \s+3.\|.\|.\s0
.I xxn
where \fIn\fR
may not be greater than 99).
These sections get the following pieces of
.IR file :
.RS 8
.TP 6
00:
From the start of
.I file
up to (but not including) the line referenced by
.IR first_arg .
.PD 0
.sp
.TP 6
01:
From the line referenced by
.I first_arg 
up to the line referenced by
.IR second_arg .
.sp
.PD 0
.TP 6
n+1:
From the line referenced by
.I last_arg 
to the end of
.IR file .
.sp
.SH OPTIONS
.TP 8
.BI \-f \ prefix
If the
.B \-f
option is used, the created files are named \fIprefix\fR00
\&\s+3.\|.\|.\s0\&
.I prefixn.
The default is \fIxx\fR00 \s+3.\|.\|.\s0
.I xxn\fR.\fP
.sp
.TP 8
.B \-k
.B Csplit
normally removes created files if an error occurs.
If the
.B \-k
option is present,
.B csplit
leaves previously created files intact.
.sp
.TP 8
.B \-s
.B Csplit
normally prints the character counts for each file created.
If the 
.B \-s
option is present,
.B csplit
suppresses the printing of all character counts.
.sp
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP 8 
.RI / rexp /
A file is to be created for the section from the current line
up to (but not including) the line containing the regular expression
.IR rexp .
The current line becomes the line containing
.IR rexp .
This argument may be followed by an optional
.B + 
or
.B \-
some number of lines
(e.g., \fB/Page/\-5\fP).
.sp
.TP 8 
.RI % rexp %
This argument is the same as
.RI / rexp /,
except that no file is created for the section.
.sp
.TP 8 
.I lnno
A file is to be created from the current line up to (but not including)
.IR lnno .
The current line becomes
.IR lnno .
.bp
.TP 8 
.RI { num }
Repeat argument.
This argument may follow any of the above arguments.
If it follows a
.I rexp
type argument, that argument is applied
.I num
more times.
If it follows
.IR lnno ,
the file will be split every
.I lnno
lines
.RI ( num\^
times)
from that point.
.sp
Enclose all
.I rexp
type arguments that contain blanks or other characters meaningful to
the Shell in the appropriate quotes.
Regular expressions may not contain embedded new-lines.
.B Csplit
does not affect the original file;
it is the users responsibility to remove it.
.sp
.SH EXAMPLES
.LP
\fBcsplit \-f cobol \fIfile\fB \(fm/procedure division/\(fm /par5./ /par16.\fR
.sp
This example creates four files (because there are three arguments:
1) procedure division 2) par5. 3) par16.):
cobol00 \|.\|.\|.\s0 cobol03.
After editing the "split" files, they can be recombined as follows:
.sp
.RS
\fBcat cobol0[0\-3] >
.I file
.RE
.sp
Note that this example overwrites the original file.
.sp
.RS
\fBcsplit -k
.I file
.B 100 {99}
.RE
.sp
This example splits the file every 100 lines, up to
10,000 lines.
The
.B \-k
option causes the created files to be retained if there
are more than 10,000 lines; however, an error message 
is printed.
.sp
.RS
\fBcsplit -k prog.c \(fm%main(%\(fm  \(fm/^}/+1\(fm {20}
.RE
.sp
Assuming that prog.c follows the normal
C coding convention of ending routines with a }
at the beginning of the line,
this example creates a file containing each separate
C routine (up to 21) in prog.c.
.sp
.SH SEE ALSO
ed(1), sh(1), regexp(7).
.sp
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Self explanatory except for:
.sp
.RS
arg \- out of range
.RE
.sp
which means that the given argument did not reference a line
between the current position and the end of the file.
